assistant: I didn't confirm the cause: no request with the new header has been sent to the live vendor yet. Typecheck and lint pass. `pnpm test` has 2 failures, both `UNMATCHED_REQUEST` because the recordings lack the new header; the other 12 tests pass. Besides the repro, the old-version (2026-01-01) `order-create.json` fixture also fails for that reason, so the claim that its output is unchanged rests on reading the code. The old recorded response already gives the expected output, and the change only adds a request header. The fix is a single edit in `src/tools/create_order.ts`.

Why I think this is the cause: the recording only shows the new vendor accepting the order and then 404ing on read-back. The only lever the spec offers is an optional `Idempotency-Key` header on `POST /orders`, "Recommended… a unique key per order attempt", and shop-mcp never sent it. If the re-record against the live vendor still returns 404, the likely real cause is a delay before new orders become readable, and the fix would have to change.

Separately, the new vendor now returns `created_at` as epoch milliseconds instead of an ISO string. `get_order` passes that to `Date.parse`, which gives NaN and falls back to 1970. That affects `get_order`, not this tool, so I left it alone.

Subject: send an Idempotency-Key when creating orders
Vendor version 2026-10-01 accepts `POST /orders` and returns 200 with an order id, but the order can't be read back: `GET /orders/{id}` returns 404. shop-mcp therefore reported order ids that don't exist. The published spec lists an optional, "recommended" `Idempotency-Key` header (a unique key per order attempt) that shop-mcp never sent. The likely cause is that the new version doesn't keep orders posted without one. `create_order` now sends a fresh `randomUUID()` as `idempotency-key` on each call, which the old version 2026-01-01 also accepts, so v1 output should stay the same. Both affected recordings need re-recording because of the new header, and that re-record will confirm whether the header is really the cause.

